{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Fix pushed as Gerrit 2275 — and I MOVED it mid-flight, which is worth explaining\n\n**Where the fix landed, and why not where I first put it.** My first version added a helper to\n`_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py`, replacing its three `str(repo_root) if repo_root else \".\"`\nfallbacks. That was the deepest correct layer — and it would have FAILED CI.\n\nThat file sits at **exactly 800 LOC**, the locked hard cap. My version took it to **823**. Every\nlocal gate passed: `make lint` clean, `make typecheck` clean across 677 files, 882 targeted tests\ngreen. The module-size gate is a step in `_build-and-test.yml`, NOT a `make lint` target, so a\ngreen local run is entirely consistent with a commit CI rejects. I only checked because a\nparallel agent hit the identical trap on the same file an hour earlier and reported it.\n`pytest tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` is the local mirror; it is not in the default\nselection.\n\nThe file had no headroom for even a one-line import, and I declined to delete a blank line to fit\n— buying space with a cosmetic diff in a file at a hard limit is the exact pattern that had just\ngone wrong.\n\n**The relocation is better on the merits, not just a workaround.**\n`llm/gate_source.resolve_gate_handle` is documented as the function that applies 'the configured\ndefaults for `None`', and it ALREADY does exactly that for `ref` and `source`:\n\n    resolved_ref    = ref    or default_ref(repo_root)\n    resolved_source = source or default_source(repo_root)\n    repo_root       = snapshot_repo_root(repo_root)      # <- the third default it owed\n\nThe root was simply a default it forgot. It is also the single entry point every attested gate\npasses through, so one change covers `review_plan`, `scan_spec`, `verify_completion` and\n`review_code`. The helper itself lives beside `repo_root_or_none` in `_config_resolvers`, the\nmodule that already owns root resolution — so the config layer resolves and the snapshot layer\nconsumes, rather than the snapshot layer reaching sideways into config.\n\n**Evidence.** The original RED reproduced the production error byte-for-byte at the snapshot\nlayer (`cannot resolve ref 'main' to a commit in '.'`). After relocating I retargeted the test to\nthe layer actually changed and established teeth by mutation: replacing the new line with `pass`\nreturns it to RED, and the negative control (an explicit `repo_root` is never overridden by\n`REBAR_ROOT`) stays green — that direction would be worse than the bug, since a gate would verify\na different tree than the caller named and still sign an attestation saying otherwise.\n\nGates: lint clean, typecheck clean (677 files), **module-size gate 4 passed**, 129 tests across\ngate_source / plan-review / snapshot consumers.\n\n**Residual, not fixed here:** `repo_snapshot.py`'s bare-cwd fallback remains for non-gate callers.\nThat is the same class as `damageable-huge-xiphosuran` and is left to it rather than forced into\na file with zero headroom.\n\n**LIVE VALIDATION still unticked** — pending 2275 merging and the container redeploying."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787863495612720001,"uuid":"29f6cee0-4cc8-4ee8-99e1-d9b8461b2991"}